Specificity:

id = 100 points
classes = 10 points
elements = 1 points

Compound selector:

class element{

}
e.g
.home p{

}

specificity will be 

10 1{

}. making it 11 points because home is 10 and element is 1 points



FLEX WRAP: contains e.g all your images that are inline to a max width
E.G
.thumbnails {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap; or nowrap or wrap reverse
    max-width: 560px;
    margin: 50px auto;
}

FLEX GROW: this is used to make an element grow to fill the available space in a flex 
container.
E.G
.thumbnails {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    max-width: 560px;
    margin: 50px auto;
    flex-grow: 1;
}

FLEX BASIS: this is used to set the initial size of an element before the remaining 
space is distributed.
E.G
.thumbnails {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    max-width: 560px;
    margin: 50px auto;
    flex-grow: 1;
    flex-basis: 200px;
}

FLEX SHRINK: this is used to set the initial size of an element before the remaining
space is distributed.
E.G
.thumbnails {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    max-width: 560px;
    margin: 50px auto;
    flex-grow: 1;
    flex-basis: 200px;
    flex-shrink: 1;
}        

overflow: hidden or visible or scroll or auto
overflow-x and overflow-y


FLOAT:

Zindex: this works in a stack context. meaning <html> will be a base and other elements will stack ontop
to make a container or an element the root e.g a div of some children within, then you have to make that
container z-index = 0 and every other items will build on top of it

em:
this inherits the default font size of the parent of the element
e.G
<h1>
    <span>hello</span>
</h1>

so h1 is parent and span is child. so if h1 has a font size of 20px, then the span will inherit that and if you set the span to 2em, 
it will be 40px. if no font size is set on the parent, then it will inherit the default font size of the browser which is 16px. 
so if you set the span to 2em, it will be 32px

in case of margin and padding, em will be based on the font size of the element itself. 
so if you set a margin of 2em on the span, it will be 40px.


@media: this is used to make your website responsive. it is a conditional statement that will apply the css rules 
within it if the condition is met. It is the if statement of css. e.g
@media (max-width: 600px) {
    .container {
        width: 100%;
    }
}
this statement will apply the css rules within it if the viewport width is less than or equal to 600px.
if (max-width <= 600px){
        .container {
        width: 100%;
    }
}

@media (min-width: 600px) {
    .container {
        width: 80%;
    }
}
this statement will apply the css rules within it if the viewport width is greater than or equal to 600px.
if (min-width >= 600px){
        .container {
        width: 80%;
    }
}

you can also combine min-width and max-width to target a specific range of viewport widths. e.g
@media (min-width: 500px) and (max-width: 799px) {
    body {
        color: aliceblue;
        background-color: cornflowerblue;
    }
}

@media (min-width: 800px) {
    h1 {
        font-size: 3.5rem;
    }    
}

Mobile first approach: this is a design approach where you design for mobile devices first 
and then scale up to larger devices.

common breakpoints for responsive design are:
320px - 480px: Mobile devices
481px - 768px: iPads, Tablets
769px - 1024px: Small screens, laptops
1025px - 1200px: Desktops, large screens
1280px and more: Extra large screens, TV


Grid: this is a layout system that allows you to create complex layouts with rows and columns.

grid-template-columns: this as the name implies specify the number of columns 
grid-template-rows: this as the name implies specify the number of rows 
gap: rows columns e.g: gap:.8em .6em

fr : fr units represents a fraction of the available space in the grid. 
e.g grid-tempplate-columns: 1fr 1fr 1fr 1fr => this means create 4 columns and ocuppy all spce evenly
e.g grid-tempplate-columns: auto 1fr 1fr 1fr  => the first column will only take what it needs why the remaining
will take up the spaces

Grids rows and implicit grid

    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    grid-template-rows: 5em 5em;
    gap: .5em;

Looking at above example we see rows  were not define how many rows there should be, we only exlicitly say 
row1 and row2 should have 5 em fixed width. and if we add more content to row 1 and two they will overflow
cause we fixed the measurement. use align-items: value??


Responsive Image Grid:

.grid-container {
    display: grid;
    grid-gap: .5em;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(5, 1fr);
    grid-template-rows: 200px; this is explicitly defined. only the first row will be 200 others will be the 75px as below
    grid-auto-rows: 75px;
}

Use auto fit to scale based on the dom dimension. 
.grid-container {
    display: grid;
    grid-gap: .5em;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, 1fr);
    grid-auto-rows: 75px;
}

however 1 fr will only add 1 column and takes up the entire of the column use the below

.grid-container {
    display: grid;
    grid-gap: .5em;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, 100px);
    grid-auto-rows: 75px;
}

since we define the size of each column then the dom will scale and increase or decrease
number of images in a row


Dense and MinMax:
The example below we manipulated explicitly some of the images and misconfigured or introduced extra space
However we can fix with grid-auto-flow: dense;

.grid-container {
    display: grid;
    grid-gap: .5em;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, 100px);
    grid-auto-rows: 75px;
    grid-auto-flow: dense;
}

.wide {
    grid-column: span 2;
}

.tall {
    grid-row: span 2;
}

.big {
     grid-row: span 2;
     grid-column: span 2;
}

Min Max :
Defines a size range fgreater than or equal to min and less than or equal to max
e.g minmax(100px, 1fr)

.grid-container {
    display: grid;
    grid-gap: .5em;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(100px, 1fr));
    grid-auto-rows: 75px;
    grid-auto-flow: dense;
}

This mean images wont go lesses than our min of 100px but will scale to fill empty spaces
But will also not go too wide cause 1fr is containing it

another example

@media(min-width: 870px){
/*
Challenge:
    1. Make sure the last 3 columns are a minimum 
       of 70px each and a maximum of 1fr.
*/
    main {
        grid-template-columns: repeat(12, 1fr);
        /* grid-template-columns: repeat(9, 1fr) repeat(3, minmax(70px, 1fr)); */
        grid-template-areas: 
            "tech tech tech tech tech tech envi envi envi nasa nasa nasa"
            "phys phys phys heal heal heal heal heal heal nasa nasa nasa"
    }
}


UNIT USAGE
rem : use rem unit for font sizes, no compounding and more predictable
margin and padding : use em unit since they  proportionally scale based on element current font size
Line height: use unitless
images and height: use percentages or view port (vh vw)
max-width: use pixel
